\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper develops a deliberately minimal model of differentiation competition to study an institutional arrangement in which local governments supply product-oriented R\&D (e.g., cultivar breeding and related branding investments) and downstream firms (cooperatives, trading firms, and jurisdiction-level marketing/branding bodies) engage in price competition using the resulting assets. The analysis delivers closed-form conditions under which decentralized public R\&D can reduce social welfare, and it provides a transparent corrective benchmark. The central step is to recognize that while jurisdictional R\&D raises consumers' perceived utility through a demand shifter (perceived quality), that shift need not map one-for-one into real welfare. We therefore decompose perceived quality using the real-quality share $\alpha$ and then leverage the closed-form downstream price equilibrium and reduced-form profits to compare the decentralized R\&D equilibrium with the social planner's allocation. The resulting comparison yields a sharp threshold: when $\alpha$ lies below $\bar\alpha=\frac{2(1-\rho)}{3(1+\rho)}$, decentralization can generate excessive investment, whereas for $\alpha$ above this threshold decentralization can generate underinvestment.

The policy implication is that one should not evaluate interjurisdictional R\&D competition uniformly. Instead, the appropriate degree and direction of coordination---support or restraint---depends on (i) whether the relevant R\&D primarily creates persuasion (demand reallocation) or real quality (a genuine welfare improvement), captured by $\alpha$, and (ii) the magnitude of knowledge spillovers, $\rho$.%
\footnote{Empirically, the dominant finding in the agricultural R\&D literature is that public research earns high rates of return---median estimates range from 40\% to over 60\% per year \citep{HurleyRaoPardey2014,AlstonPardeyRao2020}---which suggests that underinvestment (high $\alpha$, strong spillovers) is the more prevalent case for genuine research. By contrast, evidence on the returns to generic promotion is mixed and generally lower \citep{KinnucanZheng2004}. In terms of our model, this empirical landscape points to a world in which most jurisdictions sit in the {\em underinvestment} region ($\alpha>\bar\alpha$), and a positive matching rate is warranted---though the promotion component of public programs may still lie in the overinvestment region.} Put differently, the same decentralized ``race'' can be wasteful when it is mostly business stealing (low $\alpha$) but insufficient when it is mostly true quality with external benefits (high $\alpha$). Figure~\ref{fig:regions} provides a simple policy-diagnostic mapping: the region $\alpha<\bar\alpha(\rho)$ calls for disciplining instruments---a lower/possibly negative matching rate (which can be interpreted as restraint, including tax-like discipline)---whereas $\alpha>\bar\alpha(\rho)$ calls for support (a positive matching rate), and the illustrative cases discussed in the introduction can be placed on the chart depending on whether they are closer to persuasion-heavy branding or research-heavy quality improvement. In the baseline model with a single policy margin, a central government (or any effective coordination mechanism) can use a single matching instrument applied to R\&D costs as a closed-form benchmark for corrective design, implementing the constrained social optimum within that one-instrument environment. In the two-input extension that separates real research from purely persuasive promotion, decentralization can also distort the composition of policy toward persuasion; fully restoring efficiency generally requires instrument differentiation across policy components (e.g., supporting research while disciplining promotion). The analysis thus highlights the complementarity between (i) cooperative mechanisms such as the division of research tasks, joint research, and systematic sharing of outcomes, and (ii) an evaluation framework that disciplines whether public interventions are producing real quality improvements rather than merely reallocating demand through persuasive channels.

Two caveats are worth noting. First, our results are derived in a deliberately stylized two-jurisdiction Hotelling environment; we do not claim that the closed-form cutoff or the implementation formula are quantitatively robust to richer geography or additional jurisdictions. That said, the core mechanism is a general one: decentralized incentives are pushed toward overprovision by business-stealing motives when perceived quality is largely persuasive (low $\alpha$), and toward underprovision when welfare-relevant quality generates spillovers (high $\rho$); extensions to three regions or circular-city settings are expected to preserve this basic trade-off even if the exact threshold shifts.

Second, implementing $s^{*}$ is information intensive: it requires the center to assess the welfare-relevant share $\alpha$ and the spillover parameter $\rho$, which are likely hard to observe and may be mismeasured. A natural implication of the model is that misestimation can lead not only to the wrong intensity but even the wrong \emph{direction} of intervention (supporting when restraint is warranted, or restraining when support is warranted). Finally, while the formula allows ``negative matching,'' in practice this can be interpreted less as a literal tax on local R\&D and more as regulatory discipline---tight eligibility definitions, caps on subsidized spending, and disclosure/evaluation requirements that screen out persuasion-heavy programs.

Because the paper intentionally focuses on a stripped-down benchmark, several extensions are natural. First, rather than taking $\alpha$ as exogenous, one could model institutional choices---certification schemes, third-party evaluation, and disclosure requirements---that endogenously shift the relative weight of the real component, thereby clarifying the interaction between evaluation institutions and R\&D competition. Second, introducing export markets or demand from outside the two jurisdictions would bring in welfare-relevant market-expansion effects and international differentiation competition, sharpening the role of coordination, intellectual-property and branding governance, and the policy consequences of spillovers. Third, endogenizing downstream organizational forms (integration of cooperatives, competition among trading firms, and governance of collective brands) would allow a more direct analysis of the interaction between the institutional design that mitigates cannibalization and the incentives generated by interjurisdictional R\&D competition. These extensions can build directly on the closed-form building blocks derived here---the price equilibrium, reduced-form profits, decentralized and planner first-order conditions, and the implementation result---and add comparative statics and welfare evaluation in richer environments.

In sum, local-government R\&D and brand formation are potentially powerful instruments for regional differentiation; yet when they primarily operate through persuasion (business stealing), decentralized promotion policies may induce excessive and socially wasteful investment. This paper characterizes the conditions under which the direction of the distortion reverses and provides a design principle that implements the social optimum using a minimal policy instrument.
